67,139,250,.15);color:var(--purple)}.btn-success{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);color:var(--emerald)}.story-card{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(96,165,250,.06),rgba(167,139,250,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.12);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:24px;margin:0 0 16px}.story-label{font-size:.7em;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.2px;color:var(--purple);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px}.story-title{font-weight:700;font-size:1.1em;margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--text)}.story-text{font-size:.85em;line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-sec)}.story-moral{background:rgba(167,139,250,.08);border-left:3px solid var(--purple);padding:10px 14px;margin-top:12px;border-radius:0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);font-style:italic}.quote-box{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(52,211,153,.06),rgba(96,165,250,.04));border:1px solid rgba(52,211,153,.12);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin:12px 0 20px}.quote{font-size:1.05em;font-weight:600;font-style:italic;color:var(--text)}.attribution{font-size:.8em;color:var(--text-muted);margin-top:6px}.section-label{font-size:.65em;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:var(--text-muted);font-weight:700;margin:24px 0 4px}.section-divider{height:1px;background:var(--border);margin:24px 0}.q-card{background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin:12px 0}.q-text{font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px;font-size:.92em}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.03);border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;transition:.15s}.q-opt:hover{background:rgba(167,139,250,.08);border-color:var(--purple)}.q-opt.correct{background:rgba(52,211,153,.12);border-color:var(--emerald);color:var(--emerald)}.q-opt.wrong{background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);border-color:var(--red);color:var(--red)}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:12px;padding:12px 14px;background:rgba(96,165,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec)}.q-soln.show{display:block}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="index.html" style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;text-decoration:none"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="color:var(--emerald)"><path d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5z"/><path d="M2 17l10 5 10-5"/><path d="M2 12l10 5 10-5"/></svg><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Condensed Matter Physics — Crystal Structure, Band Theory, Semiconductors &amp; Superconductivity</h2><p>Deep dive into Condensed Matter Physics — <strong>crystal structure</strong> (Bravais lattices, unit cells, the 14 Bravais lattices in 3D, basis, crystal systems, Miller indices), <strong>X-ray diffraction</strong> and <strong>Bragg's law</strong> n? = 2d sin ?, <strong>reciprocal space</strong> and <strong>Brillouin zones</strong>, <strong>Bloch's theorem</strong> (?_k(r) = e^{ik·r} u_k(r) and energy bands E_n(k)), <strong>electronic band theory</strong> and the classification of solids into metals, insulators, and semiconductors, the <strong>Fermi surface</strong>, the <strong>Drude model</strong> (classical conduction with scattering time t, conductivity s = n e² t/m), the <strong>Sommerfeld model</strong> (free electron gas with Fermi-Dirac statistics, Fermi energy E_F = ?²k_F²/2m), <strong>semiconductors</strong> (intrinsic and extrinsic, n-type and p-type doping, p-n junctions, band bending, transistors), the <strong>quantum Hall effect</strong> (integer and fractional, Landau levels, topological order), and <strong>superconductivity</strong> (zero resistance, the Meissner effect, the BCS theory (Cooper pairs, the phonon-mediated attraction, the energy gap ? ~ 1.76 k T_c), type I and type II superconductors, and high-temperature superconductivity in cuprates).</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"The greatest challenge to physics today is understanding the behavior of electrons in solids."</div><div class="attribution">— Philip W. Anderson</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>?? Crystal Structure, Reciprocal Space, and Diffraction</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Lattices</div><div class="story-title">Bravais Lattices, Miller Indices, Bragg's Law, and Brillouin Zones</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>crystal</strong> is a periodic arrangement of atoms defined by a <strong>Bravais lattice</strong> R = n1a1 + n2a2 + n3a3 (a_i are primitive vectors, n_i integers) plus a <strong>basis</strong> of atoms at each lattice point. There are <strong>14 Bravais lattices</strong> in 3D, grouped into 7 crystal systems (cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, rhombohedral, monoclinic, triclinic). Common structures: <strong>simple cubic (sc)</strong>, <strong>body-centered cubic (bcc)</strong> (e.g., Fe, W), <strong>face-centered cubic (fcc)</strong> (e.g., Cu, Al, Au), <strong>hexagonal close-packed (hcp)</strong> (e.g., Mg, Ti). <strong>Miller indices (hkl)</strong>: the reciprocals of the intercepts of a plane with the axes, used to label crystal planes. The interplanar spacing d_{hkl} = a/v(h²+k²+l²) for cubic crystals. <strong>Bragg's law</strong>: n? = 2d sin ? — constructive interference of X-rays scattered by parallel planes. The <strong>reciprocal lattice</strong> vectors G = h b1 + k b2 + l b3 satisfy e^{iG·R} = 1. The <strong>Brillouin zone</strong>: the Wigner-Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice. The first Brillouin zone (FBZ) contains all inequivalent wavevectors. <strong>Laue condition</strong>: a diffraction peak occurs when ?k = G, equivalent to Bragg's law. <strong>Debye-Scherrer method</strong>: powder diffraction rings. <strong>Structure factor</strong> S(G) = S_j f_j e^{iG·r_j} determines the intensity of diffraction peaks; systematic absences (e.g., fcc: h, k, l all even or all odd) reveal the lattice type.</div><div class="story-moral">Crystals are periodic arrays of atoms; the reciprocal lattice and Brillouin zone are the natural language for describing wave propagation and diffraction in periodic media.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>?? Band Theory: Bloch Electrons and the Fermi Surface</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Bands</div><div class="story-title">Bloch's Theorem, Nearly Free Electron Model, Tight-Binding Model, and Energy Gaps</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Bloch's theorem</strong>: in a periodic potential V(r+R) = V(r), the eigenfunctions satisfy ?_k(r) = e^{ik·r} u_k(r), where u_k(r) has the periodicity of the lattice. The energy eigenvalues E_n(k) form <strong>bands</strong> indexed by n and continuous in k within the FBZ. <strong>Nearly free electron model</strong>: treat the lattice potential as a weak perturbation to the free electron gas. Energy gaps open at the Brillouin zone boundaries (k = ±G/2) due to Bragg reflection. The gap ?E = 2|V_G|, where V_G is the Fourier component of the potential. <strong>Tight-binding model</strong>: start from atomic orbitals and include hopping between neighbors. For a single s-orbital per site on a simple cubic lattice: E(k) = E_0 - 2t(cos k_x a + cos k_y a + cos k_z a), where t is the hopping integral. <strong>Effective mass</strong> m* = ?² (?²E/?k²)^{-1} characterizes the curvature of the band. <strong>Metals</strong>: the Fermi level lies inside a band (finite density of states at E_F). <strong>Insulators</strong>: a full valence band separated by a large energy gap E_g > 3 eV from an empty conduction band. <strong>Semiconductors</strong>: E_g < 3 eV; at finite temperature, electrons are thermally excited across the gap. <strong>Direct vs. indirect band gap</strong>: in direct gap (GaAs), the conduction band minimum and valence band maximum are at the same k; in indirect gap (Si), they are at different k values. The <strong>Fermi surface</strong> is the constant-energy surface E(k) = E_F. Its topology determines transport properties. The <strong>density of states</strong> g(E) = (1/V) S_{n,k} d(E - E_n(k)) is fundamental for thermodynamic and transport coefficients.</div><div class="story-moral">Periodic potentials create band structure: energy bands separated by gaps. The position of the Fermi level relative to bands determines whether a solid is a metal, insulator, or semiconductor.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>?? Semiconductors: Doping, p-n Junctions, and Devices</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Devices</div><div class="story-title">Intrinsic and Extrinsic Semiconductors, p-n Junctions, and Transistors</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Intrinsic semiconductors</strong> (pure Si, Ge): the carrier concentration n_i = p_i = v(N_c N_v) exp(-E_g/2kT). N_c = 2(2p m_e* kT/h²)^{3/2}, N_v = 2(2p m_h* kT/h²)^{3/2}. <strong>Extrinsic doping</strong>: adding donor atoms (Group V: P, As, Sb) creates <strong>n-type</strong> material with extra electrons. Acceptor atoms (Group III: B, Al, Ga) create <strong>p-type</strong> material with holes. The Fermi level shifts: E_F = E_c - kT ln(N_c/N_d) for n-type, E_F = E_v + kT ln(N_v/N_a) for p-type. The <strong>p-n junction</strong>: when p-type and n-type materials meet, carriers diffuse across the junction, creating a <strong>depletion region</strong> (width W = v(2e(V_bi - V)/(e)(1/N_a + 1/N_d))). The <strong>built-in potential</strong> V_bi = (kT/e) ln(N_a N_d / n_i²). Under <strong>forward bias</strong>, current flows exponentially: I = I_0 (exp(eV/kT) - 1). Under <strong>reverse bias</strong>, a small saturation current flows. The <strong>diode equation</strong>: I = I_0 (e^{eV/nkT} - 1). The <strong>bipolar junction transistor (BJT)</strong>: three layers (n-p-n or p-n-p), current amplification with ß = I_C/I_B. The <strong>field-effect transistor (FET)</strong>: voltage-controlled current through a channel between source and drain, modulated by the gate voltage. The <strong>MOSFET</strong> is the building block of modern integrated circuits. <strong>Moore's law</strong>: transistor density doubles ~every two years. <strong>Heterostructures</strong>: layers of different semiconductors (e.g., GaAs/AlGaAs) create quantum wells, leading to the <strong>quantum Hall effect</strong> and <strong>two-dimensional electron gases (2DEGs)</strong>.</div><div class="story-moral">Doping introduces extra carriers; p-n junctions are the foundation of semiconductor devices—diodes, transistors, and integrated circuits that power modern electronics.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>?? Superconductivity and the Quantum Hall Effect</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Quantum</div><div class="story-title">BCS Theory, Type I and II Superconductors, the Meissner Effect, and High-T_c Cuprates</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Superconductivity</strong> was discovered by Onnes (1911) in mercury at 4.2 K. Two defining properties: <strong>zero DC electrical resistance</strong> and the <strong>Meissner effect</strong> (expulsion of magnetic flux, perfect diamagnetism). <strong>BCS theory</strong> (Bardeen, Cooper, Schrieffer, 1957): electrons form <strong>Cooper pairs</strong> via an attractive interaction mediated by lattice vibrations (phonons). The effective interaction: V_{eff}(q, ?) = |M_q|² ?_q²/(?² - ?_q²). At the Fermi level, the exchange of virtual phonons creates an attractive interaction, binding electrons into pairs of opposite spin and momentum (k?, -k?). The BCS <strong>ground state</strong>: |BCS? = ?_k (u_k + v_k c†_{k?}c†_{-k?}) |0?. The <strong>gap equation</strong>: ?_k = -S_{k'} V_{kk'} ?_{k'}/(2E_{k'}) where E_k = v(?_k² + ?²). The <strong>superconducting gap</strong> at T=0: ?0 = 2??_D exp(-1/N(0)V). The critical temperature: kT_c = 1.14 ??_D exp(-1/N(0)V). The ratio 2?0/kT_c ˜ 3.53. <strong>Type I superconductors</strong> (pure elements): complete Meissner effect, critical field H_c ~ 10²-10³ G. <strong>Type II superconductors</strong> (alloys, high-T_c): partial flux penetration in the <strong>mixed state</strong> with flux vortices between H_{c1} and H_{c2}. <strong>High-temperature superconductivity</strong> discovered by Bednorz and Müller (1986) in La2-_xBa_xCuO4 (T_c ~ 35 K), followed by YBa2Cu3O7-d (T_c ~ 92 K) and Bi2Sr2Ca2Cu3O10 (T_c ~ 110 K). The mechanism of high-T_c is not definitively settled — likely involves spin fluctuations rather than phonons. The <strong>integer quantum Hall effect</strong> (von Klitzing, 1980): ?_xy = h/(e² ?) with ? an integer, plateaus in ?_xy at precise values determined by the fine-structure constant. <strong>Fractional quantum Hall effect</strong> (Tsui, Stormer, Gossard, 1982): plateaus at rational ? = p/q (e.g., 1/3, 2/5), explained by the <strong>Laughlin wavefunction</strong> and composite fermions.</div><div class="story-moral">Superconductivity arises from Cooper pairing mediated by phonons (BCS); the integer and fractional quantum Hall effects reveal topological order in two-dimensional electron systems.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">?? Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">?? Reset All</button><a href="physics.html" class="btn btn-success">? Back to Physics Map</a></div></div></div><script>
var practiceQs=[{id:45133,text:"?? The 14 Bravais lattices in 3D are grouped into how many crystal systems?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"7",c:true},{l:"B",t:"6",c:false},{l:"C",t:"14",c:false},{l:"D",t:"4",c:false}],sol:"There are <strong>7 crystal systems</strong> (cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, rhombohedral, monoclinic, triclinic) and 14 Bravais lattices."},{id:45134,text:"?? Bragg's law for X-ray diffraction is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"n? = 2d sin ?",c:true},{l:"B",t:"n? = d sin ?",c:false},{l:"C",t:"n? = 2d cos ?",c:false},{l:"D",t:"n? = d cos ?",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Bragg's law</strong>: n? = 2d sin ?, where d is the interplanar spacing, ? is the glancing angle, and n is the order of reflection."},{id:45135,text:"?? The first Brillouin zone is the Wigner-Seitz cell of:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The reciprocal lattice",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The direct lattice",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The unit cell",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The structure factor",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>first Brillouin zone</strong> is the Wigner-Seitz primitive cell of the reciprocal lattice. It contains all inequivalent wavevectors k."},{id:45136,text:"?? Bloch's theorem states that wavefunctions in a periodic potential have the form:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"?_k(r) = e^{ik·r} u_k(r)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"?_k(r) = e^{-ik·r} u_k(r)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"?_k(r) = sin(k·r) u_k(r)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"?_k(r) = u_k(r)",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Bloch's theorem</strong>: ?_k(r) = e^{ik·r} u_k(r), where u_k(r+R) = u_k(r) has the periodicity of the lattice."},{id:45137,text:"?? In a metal, the Fermi level lies:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Inside a band",c:true},{l:"B",t:"In the band gap",c:false},{l:"C",t:"At the bottom of the conduction band",c:false},{l:"D",t:"At the vacuum level",c:false}],sol:"In <strong>metals</strong>, the Fermi level lies within a partially filled band, giving a finite density of states at E_F and high conductivity."},{id:45138,text:"?? In the tight-binding model, the band width is proportional to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The hopping integral t",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The on-site energy E0",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The temperature",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The lattice constant",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>band width</strong> in the tight-binding model is proportional to the hopping integral t. Larger t gives wider bands (more electron delocalization)."},{id:45139,text:"?? An n-type semiconductor is created by doping silicon with:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Phosphorus (Group V)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Boron (Group III)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Silicon (Group IV)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Germanium (Group IV)",c:false}],sol:"<strong>n-type doping</strong>: Group V donors (P, As, Sb) provide extra electrons. Group III acceptors (B, Al, Ga) create p-type material with holes."},{id:45140,text:"?? The width of the depletion region in a p-n junction depends on:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The doping concentrations and applied voltage",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Only the temperature",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Only the band gap",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The mobility of carriers",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>depletion width</strong> W = v(2e(V_bi - V)/(e)(1/N_a + 1/N_d)) depends on doping concentrations, built-in potential, and applied bias."},{id:45141,text:"?? The current in a forward-biased p-n junction diode varies as:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I ? exp(eV/kT)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"I ? V",c:false},{l:"C",t:"I ? V²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"I ? ln V",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>diode equation</strong>: I = I0(exp(eV/nkT) - 1). Under forward bias, I ? exp(eV/kT) — the current increases exponentially with voltage."},{id:45142,text:"?? The BCS theory explains superconductivity through the formation of:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Cooper pairs mediated by phonons",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Single electron states",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Magnetic domains",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Fermi liquid theory",c:false}],sol:"<strong>BCS theory</strong>: electrons form Cooper pairs via phonon-mediated attraction. The paired electrons are correlated over the coherence length ? ~ 10²-104 Å."},{id:45143,text:"?? The Meissner effect is the:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Expulsion of magnetic flux from a superconductor",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Entry of magnetic flux into a superconductor",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Zero electrical resistance",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Transition temperature",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Meissner effect</strong>: a superconductor in the Meissner state expels all magnetic flux (perfect diamagnetism, B = 0 inside)."},{id:45144,text:"?? The fractional quantum Hall effect is explained by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The Laughlin wavefunction and composite fermions",c:true},{l:"B",t:"BCS theory",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The Drude model",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Tight-binding model",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>fractional QHE</strong> (? = 1/3, 2/5, etc.) is described by the <strong>Laughlin wavefunction</strong> and the theory of composite fermions — each electron binds an even number of flux quanta."}];
